WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.282 position:50% size:92% align:center
The five starting values live in a CPU array in

00:00:04.282 --> 00:00:07.885 position:50% size:92% align:center
system RAM. GPU execution needs a device input

00:00:07.885 --> 00:00:10.392 position:50% size:92% align:center
array and a device output array.

00:00:10.612 --> 00:00:12.421 position:50% size:92% align:center
Follow one value across these

00:00:12.421 --> 00:00:13.919 position:50% size:92% align:center
separate memory domains.

00:00:15.939 --> 00:00:19.233 position:50% size:92% align:center
cudaMalloc reserves device storage. Reserving an

00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:22.253 position:50% size:92% align:center
address does not copy the CPU input into it.

00:00:22.473 --> 00:00:25.954 position:50% size:92% align:center
Five FP32 values need twenty bytes in each

00:00:25.954 --> 00:00:28.937 position:50% size:92% align:center
device array. FP32 means a four-byte

00:00:28.937 --> 00:00:31.092 position:50% size:92% align:center
floating-point value here.

00:00:33.112 --> 00:00:36.311 position:50% size:92% align:center
The host-to-device copy fills the GPU input

00:00:36.311 --> 00:00:39.882 position:50% size:92% align:center
array from the CPU array. The original CPU array

00:00:39.882 --> 00:00:41.816 position:50% size:92% align:center
remains a separate object.

00:00:42.036 --> 00:00:44.697 position:50% size:92% align:center
The kernel receives the address of the device

00:00:44.697 --> 00:00:46.175 position:50% size:92% align:center
array, not the CPU array.

00:00:48.195 --> 00:00:51.141 position:50% size:92% align:center
An SM executes the thread instructions. The

00:00:51.141 --> 00:00:54.224 position:50% size:92% align:center
thread reads its device input, multiplies the

00:00:54.224 --> 00:00:56.621 position:50% size:92% align:center
value and writes the device output.

00:00:56.841 --> 00:00:59.902 position:50% size:92% align:center
A CPU output variable has not received that

00:00:59.902 --> 00:01:01.682 position:50% size:92% align:center
result merely because the

00:01:01.682 --> 00:01:03.391 position:50% size:92% align:center
GPU wrote its own array.

00:01:05.411 --> 00:01:08.427 position:50% size:92% align:center
The supplied device-to-host copy fills the CPU

00:01:08.427 --> 00:01:10.723 position:50% size:92% align:center
output array before main prints it.

00:01:10.943 --> 00:01:14.011 position:50% size:92% align:center
This copy connects GPU-produced values to CPU

00:01:14.011 --> 00:01:17.285 position:50% size:92% align:center
observation. It is a separate data movement from

00:01:17.285 --> 00:01:18.921 position:50% size:92% align:center
the kernel’s arithmetic.

00:01:20.941 --> 00:01:24.203 position:50% size:92% align:center
Consider an input array containing nine FP32

00:01:24.203 --> 00:01:27.316 position:50% size:92% align:center
values. Pause and predict the size of each

00:01:27.316 --> 00:01:29.392 position:50% size:92% align:center
device array and the size of

00:01:29.392 --> 00:01:31.245 position:50% size:92% align:center
each complete array copy.

00:01:31.465 --> 00:01:33.232 position:50% size:92% align:center
Does doubling the values require

00:01:33.232 --> 00:01:34.943 position:50% size:92% align:center
doubling that byte count again?

00:01:40.763 --> 00:01:43.710 position:50% size:92% align:center
Each nine-value array needs thirty-six bytes.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.592 position:50% size:92% align:center
The input copy and the result copy each move

00:01:46.592 --> 00:01:48.229 position:50% size:92% align:center
thirty-six payload bytes.

00:01:48.449 --> 00:01:51.962 position:50% size:92% align:center
Multiplying by two changes the values, not how

00:01:51.962 --> 00:01:55.551 position:50% size:92% align:center
many FP32 elements the arrays hold. Allocation,

00:01:55.551 --> 00:01:58.988 position:50% size:92% align:center
copying and computation solve different jobs.
